From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jert <joseph96tb@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 00:35:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: qcom: lmh: support the msm8998 limiter, thermal
 subfunction only

msm8998 has the same TrustZone DCVS limiter, reached through the same
SCM call this driver already makes: the vendor's own driver for it,
drivers/thermal/msm_lmh_dcvs.c in android-msm-wahoo-4.4, builds the
identical five-word payload under SCM_SVC_LMH / 0x10 and uses the same
node ids, subfunction ids and threshold constants. Nothing new is needed
on the firmware side.

It does differ in what it turns on. The vendor arms the thermal
subfunction alone -- one msm_lmh_dcvs_write() at probe, "Enable the
thermal algorithm early" -- and never asks for the current, reliability
or BCL algorithms, and never calls the profile change. Split the match
data so a platform can say so, rather than making msm8998 issue four
calls its shipping kernel never issues.

Without this the limiter is simply never armed: it is the layer that
acts first and fastest on this SoC, capping the OSM in hardware from
65 C junction, and Linux's own thermal zones do not start until far
above that.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
 drivers/thermal/qcom/lmh.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thermal/qcom/lmh.c b/drivers/thermal/qcom/lmh.c
index 3d072b7a4a6d..b25efa218db2 100644
--- a/drivers/thermal/qcom/lmh.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/qcom/lmh.c
@@ -30,7 +30,15 @@
 
 #define LMH_REG_DCVS_INTR_CLR		0x8
 
-#define LMH_ENABLE_ALGOS		1
+/*
+ * Which of the firmware's algorithms to turn on. sdm845 asks for all four and
+ * then changes profile; msm8998's TZ takes the same DCVSh calls but the vendor
+ * driver for it -- drivers/thermal/msm_lmh_dcvs.c in android-msm-wahoo-4.4 --
+ * arms the thermal subfunction alone and never changes profile, so that is
+ * what LMH_ENABLE_THERMAL does.
+ */
+#define LMH_ENABLE_THERMAL		1
+#define LMH_ENABLE_ALGOS		(LMH_ENABLE_THERMAL | 2)
 
 struct lmh_hw_data {
 	void __iomem *base;
@@ -140,9 +148,9 @@ static int lmh_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	}
 
 	/*
-	 * Only sdm845 has lmh hardware currently enabled from hlos. If this is needed
-	 * for other platforms, revisit this to check if the <cpu-id, node-id> should be part
-	 * of a dt match table.
+	 * Both SoCs that enable this from hlos -- sdm845 and msm8998 -- put the
+	 * first CPU of cluster 0 at 0 and of cluster 1 at 4. If a platform ever
+	 * differs, the <cpu-id, node-id> pairing belongs in the match data.
 	 */
 	if (cpu_id == 0) {
 		node_id = LMH_CLUSTER0_NODE_ID;
@@ -158,7 +166,7 @@ static int lmh_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 
 	enable_alg = (uintptr_t)of_device_get_match_data(dev);
 
-	if (enable_alg) {
+	if (enable_alg & ~LMH_ENABLE_THERMAL) {
 		ret = qcom_scm_lmh_dcvsh(LMH_SUB_FN_CRNT, LMH_ALGO_MODE_ENABLE, 1,
 					 LMH_NODE_DCVS, node_id, 0);
 		if (ret)
@@ -173,14 +181,18 @@ static int lmh_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 					 LMH_NODE_DCVS, node_id, 0);
 		if (ret)
 			dev_err(dev, "Error %d enabling BCL subfunction\n", ret);
+	}
 
+	if (enable_alg & LMH_ENABLE_THERMAL) {
 		ret = qcom_scm_lmh_dcvsh(LMH_SUB_FN_THERMAL, LMH_ALGO_MODE_ENABLE, 1,
 					 LMH_NODE_DCVS, node_id, 0);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(dev, "Error %d enabling thermal subfunction\n", ret);
 			return ret;
 		}
+	}
 
+	if (enable_alg & ~LMH_ENABLE_THERMAL) {
 		ret = qcom_scm_lmh_profile_change(0x1);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(dev, "Error %d changing profile\n", ret);
@@ -232,6 +244,7 @@ static int lmh_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 }
 
 static const struct of_device_id lmh_table[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "qcom,msm8998-lmh", .data = (void *)LMH_ENABLE_THERMAL},
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,sc8180x-lmh", },
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,sdm845-lmh", .data = (void *)LMH_ENABLE_ALGOS},
 	{ .compatible = "qcom,sm8150-lmh", },
